{:db/id 17592186129261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186128836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/id #uuid "418bea54-7fb3-5013-b91c-355a45af08e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11151" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\nx = {y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{y^2}3, \\qquad y=\\dfrac2x, \\qquad y=\\dfrac4x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/d4/d408e7580bef6b1127da124a78df019bca51a55c58968da262540cd45c1658d0.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/e6/e61d4153ecfb2fb176c06f67efb13af205b9b43b0ce4791627fcde979d96610e.svg)\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u= \\dfrac{y^2}{x}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\quad 2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{x}{3y^2} = -\\dfrac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048251} {:db/id 17592186050207} {:db/id 17592186050546}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186129504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c2703af-9466-5bfe-b4be-6b83ac22c669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186126525} {:answer/id #uuid "cf58a05e-6426-5bb8-8231-daefdfe1b35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186126526} {:answer/id #uuid "e030ad9f-a5b5-582f-939f-64d484910356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186126527} {:answer/id #uuid "3310ee92-258d-50a0-8117-218100854be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186126528} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3f2756-8915-5175-ae43-ff1f5dcb79fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186126529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf58a05e-6426-5bb8-8231-daefdfe1b35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186126526} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace18637-bd27-5171-b992-40bdb4d2ac91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186126524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297a0864-bd1f-5e14-96f0-53d43c9309da" :question/math-academy-id "q-160365" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x,\\,v=y+x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2x-1,\\,y=2x+1,\\,y=1-x,\\,y=2-x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-2x,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=y-2x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2x-1 & ⟹\\, & y-2x=-1 & ⟹\\, & u=-1 \\\\  & y=2x+1 & ⟹\\, & y-2x=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y+x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1-x & ⟹\\, & y+x=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=2-x & ⟹\\, & y+x=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/21/2116f558e3712a08c13d49238054336dcb60efc7d9602bed220be8115d392be7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186129506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2aafe7e-6c91-5d6d-8464-7dba52b30e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln 2" :db/id 17592186126537} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd35985-e84f-5813-8588-98b9055133fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\ln 2" :db/id 17592186126538} {:answer/id #uuid "d2697d0b-ab20-5ad0-9fbf-426d7b8ac704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 4" :db/id 17592186126539} {:answer/id #uuid "0d245dc4-6278-59dd-ac0e-565b7ead760b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id 17592186126540} {:answer/id #uuid "45b03faf-db45-58e5-91f3-c3f8035ce775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2" :db/id 17592186126541}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5dc97d-8128-54b5-935f-d17d396d9b2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186126536}] :question/id #uuid "af55d298-694f-59ec-ab70-d9760a4b8e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160379" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$u = \\frac{y}{x^{2}}, v = xy$,\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n$y = x^{2}, y = 2x^{2}, y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{4}{x}$."} {:db/id 17592186371899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "184368e1-d4ec-53a7-b724-26ecbd05d7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id 17592186371901} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd23822-1245-54b3-b063-1a3e5b75308f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id 17592186371902} {:answer/id #uuid "04872f3c-0156-57be-a135-c6b36ffb10ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id 17592186371903} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd73d74-b8b1-5322-8406-42b2b8fdfb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id 17592186371904} {:answer/id #uuid "8428d403-ae08-501f-9369-f40eb9366d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id 17592186371905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8428d403-ae08-501f-9369-f40eb9366d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id 17592186371905} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba19557e-03f5-528c-ad20-5f4ba059cdd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186371900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5d19e11-2889-5a4f-a620-f1b6f728a9db" :question/math-academy-id "q-160870" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-3x,\\,v=y+2x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=3x-2,\\,y=3x+2,\\,y=1-2x,\\,y=3-2x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-3x,\\,v=y+2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=y-3x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=3x+2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y+2x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=3-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9c17c81b4abc0d4ebd1eadfe951b0eb0c2674d9eb07b785e7fd972fd00e513ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186371913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d59625-eac9-589e-ab14-341551eea08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186371915} {:answer/id #uuid "7b889d37-5d45-5818-9d27-40379a4533ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186371916} {:answer/id #uuid "b06248ff-2151-5554-b5af-ab842ba5705a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id 17592186371917} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e1a6f5-fc15-5138-88ce-fdee68bb88d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id 17592186371918} {:answer/id #uuid "5b52d6ac-ffd9-5f34-ba9e-30bc4a86d030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186371919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b889d37-5d45-5818-9d27-40379a4533ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186371916} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ac9b17-c604-511f-9b48-3b87989affec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186371914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75940fb8-f294-5aab-8f7b-cfe4e75211a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160891" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{3x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{xy}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{2x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05d2ca47db245b147cb52f95a474a60ece144931c8b6e1e40c6628a99b53d690.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y} & -\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy & \\text{ }\\text{ }{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=y=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}=\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186491983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b2bbcb8-c26d-5006-a73c-bbb060d2cd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186491568} {:answer/id #uuid "aba8c641-4582-5c5c-96f9-0d22c8330b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186491753} {:answer/id #uuid "d6091ef9-6288-59c1-9465-7c82caaeb2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id 17592186491819} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb91197-701b-57ed-96ae-159bf0a24d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186491858} {:answer/id #uuid "edc9ced6-e6c7-5f1d-be8e-a811c81b1ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186491862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b2bbcb8-c26d-5006-a73c-bbb060d2cd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186491568} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c5a083c-f900-50bb-ab0f-b4084bf566f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186491899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "018796a4-bd8d-5016-90ff-54138b129ff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-160875" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=1+\\frac{x}{2},\\,y=-1+\\frac{x}{2},\\,y=x-1,\\,y=x-2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=2y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=-1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y-x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=x-1 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-1 & ⟹\\, & v=-1 \\\\  & y=x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & v=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e9/e9ad855bcb5133e9a51d990ea25d0e23c8da88c59e15825f3ea1bfc47335dc33.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186491996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f427ed-2d2a-5dfa-8945-d1b3f1c00922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186491456} {:answer/id #uuid "3d405b8d-b749-5fcf-9281-fe13658b158d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491572} {:answer/id #uuid "947befcc-fa3a-58ec-8c95-d53e4ad8cc01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491708} {:answer/id #uuid "c853fb0b-11f2-5a9b-a138-764447d95351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491794} {:answer/id #uuid "c9995f65-1329-5eb2-aafe-12e03a218173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186491797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9995f65-1329-5eb2-aafe-12e03a218173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186491797} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3443c26-bcc5-576e-a59c-ebc351a5027e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186491974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19d01b49-3d16-5ae7-a700-51548665fd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160893" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2},\\,y=3{x}^{2},\\,x={y}^{2},\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=3{x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/26/26eaba24af54a40f59f92148c145285ba4097ce765aee8cfc9a6241ff88b9498.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3{y}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}+1)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186492063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab4b08a-d977-567f-a04e-d43ed9d2d0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491542} {:answer/id #uuid "94243889-413b-535c-af6b-ce9f8dfb1709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491706} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ac3fa8-e528-579d-ad93-0c93644320df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491767} {:answer/id #uuid "e10f8c74-4f85-5c66-896d-c5e8857bd026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491836} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ff714-7cca-5ae3-9331-6463299a095b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ab4b08a-d977-567f-a04e-d43ed9d2d0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186491542} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fcb4a03-8da4-5422-b391-06e26dc1be4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186491950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcce5a2c-ee13-5e17-858d-468f144adb26" :question/math-academy-id "q-160892" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3},\\,y=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}